\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Compact gauge groups and representation data}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Replacing a finite gauge group by a compact group changes edge
sums into normalized Haar integrals. Character orthogonality survives,
but an infinite character expansion needs a convergence argument before
it can be inserted into a surface partition function. The distinction
between an #{L^2} expansion and an absolutely convergent kernel is
therefore part of the model.}

\p{For the circle group, Wilson and Villain weights give explicit
Fourier coefficients. Matching one coefficient does not match the
whole law. For matrix groups, a separate smooth-field calculation
explains the classical small-spacing normalization of the Wilson
action. Neither calculation constructs a quantum continuum limit.}

\p{Unit quaternions identify the compact group #{SU(2)} and its
binary polyhedral subgroups. A representation datum then specifies
which group acts on which field and whether it also moves the
substrate. These choices precede any physical comparison.}

